Writing standard — references/controlled-prose.md

That file is a language-neutral rule set for technical documentation. It draws
on two sources with different terms:

ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English — © AeroSpace and Defence Industries
Association of Europe (ASD). https://asd-ste100.org
  Published free for adoption. Its Part 1 writing rules (Sections 1 thru 9) are
  restated in superdev's own words. Part 2 (the Dictionary) is copyrighted and
  is NOT reproduced or bundled. ASD-STE100 is not an open-source work; obtain
  the specification from asd-ste100.org.

tekom Praxisleitfaden "Deutsch für die Technische Kommunikation — Regeln und
ihre Anwendung" — © tcworld GmbH. https://www.tekom.de
  PRIOR ART ONLY. Its notice forbids reproduction, translation, and storage in
  electronic media, so no text in controlled-prose.md derives from it. Where its
  catalogue reaches a rule ASD-STE100 does not, that rule is written here from
  the underlying principle. Cited because the two catalogues independently
  converge, which is the evidence that the shared rules are language-neutral
  rather than English-specific.

The two-mode split (strict for procedures and safety text, relaxed for general
prose) and the self-check discipline follow the distillation published by
woosal1337 — https://github.com/woosal1337/blog (videos/ep01-the-cure-for-ai-slop).

