references/content-types.md — the content-type lens — and several rules in
references/controlled-prose.md draw on:

"Technical Writing 101: A Real-World Guide to Planning and Writing Technical
Content", 3rd edition — © 2000-2009 Scriptorium Publishing Services, Inc.
Alan S. Pringle and Sarah S. O'Keefe. https://www.scriptorium.com/books
  PRIOR ART ONLY. No text is reproduced. Its Chapter 6 names the four content
  types (interface, reference, conceptual, procedural) and identifies conceptual
  information as the hardest to write and the most often absent; that taxonomy is
  restated in superdev's own words in content-types.md. Its Chapter 7 supplies the
  procedure-element rules (state a step's result, never number a result, one
  lead-in pattern across sibling procedures, one action per step) and its
  Chapter 12 the table-heading rule, each written from the principle in
  controlled-prose.md. Its Chapter 9 ranking of documentation qualities is the
  basis for doc-reader's severity order.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Five rules in references/controlled-prose.md were extended from:

"Handbook of Technical Writing", 12th edition — © 2020 Bedford/St. Martin's,
Macmillan Learning. Gerald J. Alred, Walter E. Oliu, and Charles T. Brusaw.
ISBN 978-1-319-36220-1
  PRIOR ART ONLY. No text is reproduced. Its entries on headings, definition,
  lists, dangling modifiers, and voice supply five rules written in
  controlled-prose.md from the underlying principle: a heading may not carry
  meaning the text omits, a list closes with a sentence that refers back to it,
  a definition names its category and does not restate its own term, a modifying
  phrase attaches to the word it describes, and one sentence keeps one voice.
  Cited as a third independent catalogue. It converges on the shared core rules
  from a native-English business and technical writing tradition, and it diverges
  on semicolons, dashes, sentence variety, and emphasis, where controlled-prose.md
  follows ASD-STE100 instead.

